---
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
// Order set by Zachary on 25 September 2026: bed sensor repair, filament shelf, towel hook, then the Accord.
const notes = [
  {
    image: 'images/design-notes/printer-bed-sensors.jpg',
    width: 1050, height: 1400,
    alt: 'The underside of a 3D printer bed lifted out of the machine, showing the four corner bed sensors, the wiring harness running back to a small board, and a precision screwdriver resting on the build plate.',
    title: 'Bed sensor repair, FlashForge Adventurer 5M',
    body: 'The bed level sensors failed, so the printer stopped getting a probe measurement and kept driving the head into the plate. I stripped it down, cleaned the boards and connections, and retightened the belts. Reinstalling the pressure sensors was the real problem: with no factory torque spec I could not reproduce the original preload, so rather than guess I ran a system recalibration and let the machine re-zero against the torque I had actually applied. It has printed correctly since.',
  },
  {
    image: 'images/design-notes/filament-shelf.jpg',
    width: 1400, height: 1050,
    alt: 'Wall-mounted filament shelf: two printed black brackets carrying a pair of wooden dowels with two filament spools on them, above a 3D printer.',
    title: 'Filament shelf brackets',
    body: 'I found a filament holder design online, liked the idea, and modelled my own rather than printing theirs. The original was built around 28 mm timber, which is not a size a US hardware store sells, so the bracket had to be sized around dowels I could actually buy. A small printed collar checked the dowel fit before I committed to a pair of brackets.',
  },
  {
    image: 'images/design-notes/towel-hook.jpg',
    width: 1198, height: 1400,
    alt: 'Render of a towel hook with a curved back face, shaped to sit against a curved drawer front.',
    title: 'Towel hook for a curved drawer',
    body: 'The bathroom drawer front is curved, and the generic hooks we were using only ever touched it in one place. I scanned the drawer, took the curvature off the scan, and modelled a hook whose back face follows it, so it sits against the front instead of rocking on it.',
  },
  {
    image: 'images/design-notes/accord-amp-wiring.jpg',
    width: 1306, height: 1400,
    alt: 'Two car amplifiers mounted on a carpeted panel, a four-channel Rockford Fosgate and a smaller MTX mono amp, with the wiring routed in cable-tied runs to distribution blocks.',
    title: 'Amp wiring and headliner, 2009 Honda Accord',
    body: 'In my Accord I wired a four-channel amp for the door speakers and a mono amp for the sub. When the connections in the Rockford Fosgate amp stopped working, I took it apart and cleaned it inside, and it worked fine afterward. The headliner was sagging, so I took it out, wire-brushed off the gooey residue the old material left behind, and covered it in new suede.',
  },
];
---
<ul class="design-notes">
  {notes.map((note) => (
    <li class="design-note">
      <figure class="design-note-thumb">
        <img src={`${base}${note.image}`} width={note.width} height={note.height} alt={note.alt} loading="lazy" />
      </figure>
      <div class="design-note-copy">
        <h3>{note.title}</h3>
        <p class="muted">{note.body}</p>
      </div>
    </li>
  ))}
</ul>
